<?xml version="1.0"?>
<robot xmlns:xacro="http://ros.org/wiki/xacro">

  <link name="${car_name}/imu">
    <visual>
      <origin xyz="0 0 0" rpy="0 0 0"/>
      <geometry>
        <box size="0.05 0.05 0.05"/>
      </geometry>
      <material name="Red"/>
    </visual>
  </link>

  <!-- VESC 6 MkVI onboard IMU. Yaw -90 deg; no roll.

       The board is mounted turned 90 deg CLOCKWISE from the car's forward
       direction, so the imu frame is -90 about z from base_link. Sign follows
       from that directly: rpy rotates child vectors into the parent.

       The chip is mounted upside down on the board, but the FIRMWARE already
       corrects for that, so the data reaching us is Z up. Measured, level and
       still, on /vesc/sensors/imu/raw:

           linear_acceleration:  x 0.139   y 0.082   z 9.910

       An accelerometer at rest reads the reaction to gravity, so +9.9 on z is
       z pointing up; a flipped frame would read -9.9. That topic comes
       straight from the driver and never passes through this file, which is
       what makes it a clean measurement of the sensor frame rather than of
       what we claim about it. Adding roll=pi here describes the chip instead
       of the data and applies the flip a second time.

       So: up = +imu.z, and the yaw rate is +angular_velocity.z. Confirmed on
       the same topic by turning the car slowly counter-clockwise - z rose to
       +0.24 rad/s and held there for the whole turn, then came back to zero.

       VESC TOOL DISAGREES AND IS NOT WRONG. Its display shows a positive yaw
       for a RIGHT turn, because it uses the aviation convention where yaw runs
       like a compass heading; ROS (REP-103) has positive yaw counter-clockwise.
       Reading that + as "the board is upside down" is what put roll=pi in here
       once. Judge this from the topic, never from the tool.

       With no roll and yaw -90 the axes are

           forward = +imu.y    left = -imu.x    up = +imu.z

       Confirm by pushing the car forwards: imu.y should go POSITIVE. (This
       spent a while at +1.5708, from believing Z faced the floor. It is worth
       knowing that Rz(+90)Rx(180) and Rz(-90) give the same forward axis, so
       that combination was self-consistent right up until the accelerometer
       showed Z was up.)

       The yaw does not reach cartographer: it tracks in this frame and
       publishes base_link, so a constant rotation between imu and laser is
       applied and then taken back off, and 2D uses only gyro z, which a yaw
       rotation leaves alone. Maps made before this was corrected are fine.
       controller_manager is different - it reads the longitudinal component
       out, so there the yaw matters.

       VESC Tool's own Imu Rotation (roll 7.4 / pitch -4.7 / yaw 0) is mounting
       tilt only. Leave the 90 out of it; this joint is where it belongs.

       This joint is the single description of the mounting. controller_manager
       reads it back through TF to rotate the IMU into the car's axes, and
       cartographer tracks in this frame, so an error here rotates the
       controller's yaw rate and tilts the map with no other symptom. -->
  <joint name="imu_joint" type="fixed">
    <parent link="${car_name}/base_link"/>
    <child link="${car_name}/imu"/>
    <origin xyz="0.07 0.0 ${wheel_radius+0.04}" rpy="0 0 -1.5708"/>
  </joint>

  <link name="${car_name}/laser">
    <visual>
      <origin xyz="0 0 0" rpy="0 0 0"/>
      <geometry>
        <mesh filename="package://albomb_description/meshes/UST-10LXB.stl" scale="0.001 0.001 0.001"/>
      </geometry>
      <material name="Blue"/>
    </visual>
  </link>

  <joint name="laser_joint" type="fixed">
    <parent link="${car_name}/base_link"/>
    <child link="${car_name}/laser"/>
    <origin xyz="0.259 0.0 ${wheel_radius+0.0768}" rpy="0 0 0"/>
  </joint>

  
</robot>
